=3D"description_content"><p>Given an integer array nums, return all the tri=
plets <code>[nums[i], nums[j], nums[k]]</code> such that <code>i !=3D j</co=
de>, <code>i !=3D k</code>, and <code>j !=3D k</code>, and <code>nums[i] + =
nums[j] + nums[k] =3D=3D 0</code>.</p>

<p>Notice that the solution set must not contain duplicate triplets.</p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p><strong class=3D"example">Example 1:</strong></p>

<pre><strong>Input:</strong> nums =3D [-1,0,1,2,-1,-4]
<strong>Output:</strong> [[-1,-1,2],[-1,0,1]]
<strong>Explanation:</strong>=20
nums[0] + nums[1] + nums[2] =3D (-1) + 0 + 1 =3D 0.
nums[1] + nums[2] + nums[4] =3D 0 + 1 + (-1) =3D 0.
nums[0] + nums[3] + nums[4] =3D (-1) + 2 + (-1) =3D 0.
The distinct triplets are [-1,0,1] and [-1,-1,2].
Notice that the order of the output and the order of the triplets does not =
matter.
</pre>

<p><strong class=3D"example">Example 2:</strong></p>

<pre><strong>Input:</strong> nums =3D [0,1,1]
<strong>Output:</strong> []
<strong>Explanation:</strong> The only possible triplet does not sum up to =
0.
</pre>

<p><strong class=3D"example">Example 3:</strong></p>

<pre><strong>Input:</strong> nums =3D [0,0,0]
<strong>Output:</strong> [[0,0,0]]
<strong>Explanation:</strong> The only possible triplet sums up to 0.
</pre>

<p>&nbsp;</p>
