n__cVmKc"><p>for starters, I found this problem hard but it really teaches =
us to keep track of our pointers and is helpful for interview prep in my op=
inion.</p>
<p>also, this tip is pythonic fyi</p>
<ol>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>looking at this array and trying to come up with even one set of three n=
umbers that add up to 3 numbers is stressful. What could make this easier? =
Sorting! Why does sorting work? Sorting takes out all the stress of having =
to pick 3 numbers that add to 0. (We'll get into this more in a second).</p=
>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>Sorting, this can be done by implementing your own quick sort function o=
r by using the .sort method. Quick sort is unstable (I used chatgpt to figu=
re out what this means) - basically it means that it doesnt prioritize keep=
ing similar elements in the same position they originally were. [2,2,2] mig=
ht all get jumbled up if sorted whereas in .sort() they keep their place. T=
his is probably a great call out in an interview (I'd bet they might not as=
k you to write out your own quick sort, but probably doesn't hurt to practi=
ce it for the shits)</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>Now that you're all sorted up, this becomes like two sum but instead of =
two pointers, you need 3 pointers. How does that work? the first pointer is=
 like an anchor. It is x in the 'for x in array nums'. While that's cooking=
, on each iteration, you're going to use two more pointers, a left and a ri=
ght one (to take advantage of the sorting we just did) to find solutions th=
at work. Say what?! Imagine we have array [3,4,5,6,7]  in that for loop men=
tioned above, the first element would be 3 and then we'd want two pointers =
one on 4 and 7. Add these two numbers to 3 to see if they all add up to 0. =
If they do, Taddah! if not, we'll need to increment the left or decrement t=
he right pointer depending on if that result is too small or too large.</p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>But wait, what about duplicates. since we anchor the first pointer, the =
second and third just need to not be the same as their proceeding elements.=
 this can be constrained with something like if (x-1) =3D=3D x: continue.</=
p>
</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">
<p>Save each solution off into an array and return.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Okay so boiling it down now</p>
<ol>
<li data-length=3D"1">sort</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">three pointers (check constraints on each pointer, ma=
ke sure things are being checked properly and ensure no duplicates).</li>
<li data-length=3D"1">return solution set</li>
</ol>
<p>I hope this helps and is a more explicit tip for those struggling. If th=
is problem makes sense, flex your muscles and try threesum closest, it's re=
ally similar. Good luck :)</p></div><span style=3D"font-size: 0px; line-hei=
